{"key": "1bc24d004771", "skip": false, "context": "A research team was building a benchmark to measure whether language models can tell evaluation scenarios from real deployment interactions. They curated chat and agentic transcripts from 61 distinct sources, including public benchmarks and real deployment logs, plus synthetic agentic data where coverage was thin. Using all available samples would produce an unbalanced dataset of over 3 million items, and they needed a manageable set that still let them separate the effects of interaction type from evaluation context.", "question": "How should the team subsample the collected data to support measuring evaluation awareness?"},
{"key": "2272ce92b0d5", "skip": false, "context": "A research team studied whether AI models can detect when they are being evaluated, which could undermine benchmark reliability. They built a 1,000-sample benchmark spanning chat and agentic settings and measured discrimination performance. Frontier models scored clearly above random chance (best AUC 0.83) but below a simple human baseline (AUC 0.92), and models could also identify evaluation purpose above chance under multiple-choice and open-ended questioning.", "question": "How strongly should the team state its conclusions about the extent of frontier models' evaluation awareness?"},
{"key": "ba3bacfe0f94", "skip": false, "context": "A research team wanted a human performance baseline to contextualize model scores on distinguishing evaluation from deployment transcripts. The authors each annotated all 1000 samples, reaching strong inter-rater agreement (ICC3 0.768, 95% CI [0.74, 0.79]), and averaged the ratings. However, the annotator pool was very small and the annotators were already familiar with the underlying datasets and with LLM evaluation methods, plausibly inflating their discrimination ability.", "question": "How should the team interpret and use this human baseline given its limitations?"},
{"key": "fedaf3b234a4", "skip": false, "context": "A research team measured not only models' ability to discriminate evaluation from deployment transcripts but also the calibration of their probability estimates. Plotting calibration curves for each model, they found mixed calibration overall, with curves consistently lying below the diagonal, meaning models systematically reported higher probabilities of a transcript being an evaluation than the true frequencies warranted.", "question": "How should the team interpret the finding that models tend to over-classify transcripts as evaluations?"},
{"key": "14c273e6592b", "skip": false, "context": "A research team analyzed models' chain-of-thought reasoning on an evaluation-identification task and categorized the cues models cited, such as memorization, synthetic inputs, and task formatting. They observed that capable models frequently reasoned that the probe question itself indicated they were being tested for evaluation awareness, and then classified the transcript as an evaluation on that basis. This raised the possibility that measured awareness partly reflects reasoning about the measurement setup rather than properties of the transcripts.", "question": "How should the team account for this meta-reasoning when interpreting the measured evaluation awareness?"},
{"key": "6f050c240402", "skip": false, "context": "A research team examined models' reasoning traces on an evaluation-identification task to understand what drove correct discrimination. They found that advanced reasoning models occasionally named the specific public benchmark a transcript came from, indicating recognition of material seen in training, though such cases were rare. This raised the question of how much of the measured discrimination reflected memorized benchmark content versus genuine situational awareness.", "question": "How should the team determine whether memorization of public benchmarks explains models' discriminative ability?"},
{"key": "f00b9b48eccd", "skip": false, "context": "A research team was designing probe questions to ask models whether a transcript came from an evaluation or a deployment, eliciting a binary choice with chain-of-thought reasoning. They knew a single prompt phrasing yields high-variance responses and that models respond differently to different phrasings and input formats, such as appending the question to the transcript versus quoting the transcript in a new context. They drafted 28 candidate prompt variations covering input format, wording, and option ordering.", "question": "How should the team select which probe questions to use in the main experiments?"},
{"key": "cdc2042791ea", "skip": false, "context": "A research team was constructing a dataset to test whether models can distinguish evaluation scenarios from real deployment interactions across chat and agentic usage. They had assembled over 3 million candidate samples from 61 sources spanning public benchmarks, real deployment logs, and synthetic agentic traces, but the pool was heavily unbalanced. They worried that without careful construction, apparent discrimination could reflect superficial distributional differences rather than true awareness of being evaluated.", "question": "How should the team construct the dataset so that discrimination reflects genuine evaluation awareness rather than superficial differences?"}
]}
